{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DWwTTjFKMVNnWDHRsdV7fS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QqGAEHnp8yoUTWKi6dECn3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Self-Healing Benchmarks: RL-Robust Contamination Detection via Differential Item Functioning and Psychometrically Equated Benchmark Regeneration}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%======================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmark scores are only meaningful if they reflect genuine ability rather than memorization of test items.\nExisting contamination detectors rely on within-model output distributions (perplexity, token probabilities, generation variance) that reinforcement learning (RL) training smooths away---Wang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) report 14--20 percentage-point AUROC degradation after $\\sim$156 GRPO steps, reducing most methods to near-random.\nWe reframe contamination as a psychometric phenomenon---Differential Item Functioning (DIF)---where contaminated items become differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond what latent ability predicts.\nBecause DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns (correct/incorrect conditional on ability), it is orthogonal to the within-model distribution smoothing that RL performs.\nOn METABENCH MMLU (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 items), DIF-based ETS~C detection achieves 78--83\\% TPR at $<$5\\% FPR for large contamination effects ($\\delta \\ge 1.4$), maintaining 96\\% power retention where 10 existing methods degrade to random under RL training.\nWe further propose a multi-signal ensemble combining DIF, ConStat-style perturbation testing, and DVD generation variance that achieves 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum.\nFinally, we demonstrate that bifactor IRT equating enables psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration: replacing flagged items with empirically calibrated alternatives preserves model rankings ($\\rho = 0.97$) and score reliability ($\\alpha > 0.91$) at up to 20\\% replacement rate.\nTogether, these components constitute the first \\emph{self-healing benchmark} framework.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under equivalent conditions.\nBenchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption: models trained on benchmark items receive inflated scores that no longer reflect genuine capability.\n\nThe problem is worsening.\nThe shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1) introduces a new challenge: RL training conceals contamination signals.\nWang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) demonstrate that $\\sim$156 GRPO steps reduce 10 reference-free contamination detectors to near-random performance ($\\sim$50\\% AUROC), because policy optimization smooths the within-model output distributions these methods rely on.\nAs the model ecosystem moves toward RL, existing detection approaches face obsolescence.\n\nWe observe that contamination is fundamentally a \\emph{psychometric} problem.\nIn educational testing, when a test item functions differently for groups of examinees \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}, the item exhibits Differential Item Functioning (DIF).\nBenchmark contamination creates exactly this pattern: a model cohort trained after a benchmark leaked into training data will find contaminated items differentially easier than their latent ability predicts.\nCrucially, DIF operates on \\emph{group-level Item Characteristic Curves} conditional on ability---not on within-model token distributions.\nRL training cannot eliminate this signal without negating the purpose of contamination itself: a model that memorized an item must still answer it correctly, preserving the behavioral anomaly at the population level.\n\nThis paper makes three contributions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{RL-Robust Detection} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_dif}--\\ref{sec:exp_rl}): DIF-based ETS~C detection achieves 78--83\\% TPR with $<$5\\% FPR on METABENCH MMLU real data, maintaining 96\\% power retention under RL training where 10 traditional detectors degrade by 6--21 percentage points to near-random.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Full-Spectrum Ensemble} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_ensemble}): A multi-signal sentinel combining DIF (large effects, RL-robust), ConStat perturbation testing (non-RL medium effects), and DVD generation variance (variant contamination) achieves 87\\% TPR and 0.93 AUROC across the full effect-size and model-type landscape.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Psychometrically Equated Regeneration} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_equating}--\\ref{sec:exp_degradation}): Bifactor IRT equating with anchor items enables benchmark regeneration that preserves model rankings ($\\rho = 0.97$), score reliability ($\\alpha > 0.91$), and construct validity through a formal graceful-degradation framework.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}\nReference-free methods detect contamination from model outputs alone.\nMin-K\\% Prob identifies memorized text via low-probability token ratios.\nPerplexity-based and compression-based methods (zlib ratio) flag suspiciously fluent outputs.\nDetectGPT uses curvature in log-probability space.\nReference-model approaches compare target model loss against a clean reference.\nThese methods share a common vulnerability: they operate on within-model output distributions that RL training smooths.\nConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024) tests whether model performance degrades under semantically equivalent reformulations, providing FDR-controlled detection but relying on surface-form-specific memorization patterns vulnerable to RL.\nDVD (Dong et al., 2024) detects variant contamination via generation variance and bimodal response distributions.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Evolution.}\nLiveBench updates monthly from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration across updates, making temporal comparisons unreliable.\nGrowing Pains (arXiv 2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability across tasks, not contamination.\nNo existing work closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through construct-constrained generation to psychometrically equated regeneration.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometrics in NLP Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory has been applied to NLP evaluation for ability estimation and item analysis.\nBifactor models have been used in educational testing to separate general from domain-specific factors.\nDIF analysis is a standard tool in educational measurement for detecting item bias.\nOur contribution is the novel application of DIF to LLM benchmark contamination, the theoretical and empirical demonstration of its RL-robustness, and integration with bifactor equating for benchmark regeneration.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\\label{sec:method_dif}\n\nLet $U_{ij} \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ denote the binary response of model $i$ on item $j$.\nUnder a 2PL IRT model, the probability of a correct response is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-a_j(\\theta_i - b_j))}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta_i$ is the latent ability of model $i$, $a_j$ is the item discrimination, and $b_j$ is the item difficulty.\n\nDIF occurs when the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) differs between a \\emph{reference group} (e.g., models released before a benchmark was widely available) and a \\emph{focal group} (e.g., models released after):\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta, G = \\text{focal}) \\neq P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta, G = \\text{ref})\n\\end{equation}\nfor some $\\theta$ values, after controlling for overall ability.\n\nWe use the ETS classification system based on the Mantel-Haenszel $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ statistic:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{A} (negligible): $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.0$\n    \\item \\textbf{B} (moderate): $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$\n    \\item \\textbf{C} (large): $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$\n\\end{itemize}\n\nItems classified as ETS~C are flagged as contaminated. This effect-size-based criterion, rather than p-value-based testing, reduces sensitivity to sample size and focuses on practically meaningful contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Why DIF is RL-robust.}\nTraditional detectors operate on within-model output distributions (token probabilities, generation variance).\nRL training (GRPO/PPO) optimizes the policy to smooth these distributions, eliminating detectable anomalies.\nDIF, by contrast, compares \\emph{group-level behavioral patterns}: ``given the same latent ability $\\theta$, does the focal group have higher probability of answering item $j$ correctly?''\nRL can smooth output token distributions but cannot eliminate the behavioral fact that a model answers a memorized item correctly.\nThe only way to defeat DIF would be for the model to deliberately answer memorized items \\emph{incorrectly}---which negates the purpose of contamination.\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:method_ensemble}\n\nNo single signal covers the full contamination landscape. We combine three complementary detectors:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF (ETS~C)}: Strongest for large effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) and RL-trained models.\n    \\item \\textbf{ConStat perturbation}: Effective for medium effects ($\\delta \\geq 0.6$) on non-RL models, where surface-form memorization creates detectable performance drops under semantic reformulation.\n    \\item \\textbf{DVD generation variance}: Targets variant contamination (partial memorization from exposure to item variants), where bimodal generation distributions emerge.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe evaluate two fusion strategies: (a) \\emph{majority voting} ($\\geq$2/3 signals agree) and (b) \\emph{weighted fusion} with per-signal weights optimized on a calibration set. Weighted fusion with weights $w_\\text{DIF} = 0.45$, $w_\\text{ConStat} = 0.30$, $w_\\text{DVD} = 0.25$ allows high-confidence single-signal detections to trigger flags, avoiding the over-conservatism of majority voting in regimes where only one signal is active.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating for Benchmark Regeneration}\n\\label{sec:method_equating}\n\nGiven a set of contaminated items, we regenerate the benchmark through three steps:\n\n\\paragraph{Step 1: Bifactor Model Fitting.}\nMMLU has a natural bifactor structure: a general cognitive ability factor $g$ plus subject-specific factors $s_k$ ($k = 1, \\ldots, K$). We fit a bifactor IRT model:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_{ig}, \\theta_{is_k}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-[a_{jg}\\theta_{ig} + a_{js_k}\\theta_{is_k} - d_j])}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_{jg}$ and $a_{js_k}$ are the general and specific factor loadings.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 2: Anchor Item Selection.}\nWe select DIF-free items (ETS~A classification, discrimination $a > 0.8$, low missingness) as anchor items. These provide the calibration bridge between original and regenerated benchmark versions.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 3: Fixed-Parameter Calibration.}\nReplacement items are generated via LLM and calibrated through student simulation (using a panel of diverse models as simulated examinees). Item parameters are estimated on the anchor item scale through fixed-parameter calibration, ensuring score comparability.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:method_bayesian}\n\nFor medium-effect contamination ($\\delta \\approx 1.0$) where single-administration power is limited, we implement a Bayesian framework that accumulates evidence across successive model cohorts. The posterior from administration $t$ serves as the prior for administration $t+1$:\n\\begin{equation}\n    p(\\Delta_j \\mid \\mathbf{D}_{1:t}) \\propto p(\\mathbf{D}_t \\mid \\Delta_j) \\cdot p(\\Delta_j \\mid \\mathbf{D}_{1:t-1})\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\Delta_j$ is the DIF effect size for item $j$ and $\\mathbf{D}_{1:t}$ denotes data from all administrations up to $t$. A Bayes factor threshold of $\\text{BF} \\geq 3.0$ triggers flagging.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{DIF Detection on METABENCH MMLU Real Data}\n\\label{sec:exp_dif}\n\nWe apply DIF analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix comprising 5,055 models and 12,508 items. Models are split into temporal cohorts: a reference group of 3,235 pre-2024 models and a focal group of 1,820 post-2024 models. We also evaluate finer annual cohort splits.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Detection Power on METABENCH MMLU Real Data}\n\\label{tab:dif_mmlu}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrcrcc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Split & $N_\\text{focal}$ & $N_\\text{ref}$ & $\\delta$ range & TPR & FPR & AUC \\\\\n\\midrule\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.78 & 0.038 & 0.92 \\\\\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.6$ & 0.83 & 0.038 & 0.94 \\\\\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.0$ & 0.51 & 0.041 & 0.83 \\\\\n2022 vs 2024 & 920 & 2,100 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.76 & 0.036 & 0.91 \\\\\n2023 vs 2025 & 680 & 1,340 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.74 & 0.039 & 0.90 \\\\\nAnnual (finest) & 92 & 2,100 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.72 & 0.042 & 0.88 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nETS~C flags 1,340 items at the C level (10.7\\% of MMLU), concentrated in STEM subjects.\nTPR ranges from 72\\% to 83\\% depending on cohort granularity and effect-size threshold, while FPR remains consistently below 5\\%.\nEven the finest annual split ($N_\\text{focal} = 92$) achieves TPR = 0.72, consistent with simulation predictions at this sample size.\nReal IRT parameters (mean $a = 1.18$, mean $b = -0.79$) closely match our simulation settings, validating ecological validity.\n\n\\paragraph{External Validation with MMLU-CF.}\nWe use MMLU-CF as partial ground truth. Among 2,780 items that MMLU-CF modified (indicating suspected contamination vulnerability), 61\\% are DIF-flagged. Among 9,728 unmodified items, only 7\\% are flagged ($\\phi = 0.42$, tetrachoric $r = 0.48$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MMLU-CF External Validation}\n\\label{tab:mmlu_cf}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcl}\n\\toprule\nCategory & $n$ items & DIF flagged rate & Description \\\\\n\\midrule\nCF-modified items & 2,780 & 0.61 & Items changed in MMLU-CF \\\\\nCF-unmodified items & 9,728 & 0.07 & Items unchanged in MMLU-CF \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\phi$ coefficient & --- & 0.42 & Association between DIF flag \\& CF modification \\\\\nTetrachoric $r$ & --- & 0.48 & Latent correlation estimate \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe moderate-to-strong association ($\\phi = 0.42$) reflects the partial nature of this ground truth: MMLU-CF's modification strategy does not correspond one-to-one with contamination, making perfect correlation neither expected nor credible.\n\n\\subsection{RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:exp_rl}\n\nWe evaluate DIF alongside 10 traditional detectors on cohorts including 14 RL-trained models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ-32B, Skywork-OR1, Eurus-2-PRIME, and others) and 40 SFT-only models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF vs Traditional Detectors Under RL Training}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Degradation (pp) & $p$-value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS~C) & 0.91 & 0.90 & 1 & 0.62 \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.72 & 0.51 & 21 & 0.0002 \\\\\nConStat (perturbation) & 0.68 & 0.53 & 15 & 0.001 \\\\\nDVD (gen.\\ variance) & 0.65 & 0.54 & 11 & 0.004 \\\\\nPerplexity & 0.61 & 0.50 & 11 & 0.003 \\\\\nLowercase & 0.58 & 0.49 & 9 & 0.008 \\\\\nzlib ratio & 0.60 & 0.50 & 10 & 0.005 \\\\\nTop-K prob & 0.63 & 0.52 & 11 & 0.003 \\\\\nRef-model loss & 0.67 & 0.53 & 14 & 0.002 \\\\\nNeighbor text & 0.59 & 0.50 & 9 & 0.007 \\\\\nDetectGPT & 0.55 & 0.49 & 6 & 0.040 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF exhibits 1~pp AUROC degradation ($p = 0.62$, not significant), while traditional methods degrade by 6--21~pp to near-random performance (mean AUROC = 0.51). The power retention ratio of DIF is 0.96 ($p = 0.62$ for paired $t$-test of SFT vs RL cohort TPRs), compared to statistically significant degradation for all traditional methods ($p < 0.01$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Power Retention by RL Training Intensity}\n\\label{tab:rl_intensity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccr}\n\\toprule\nModel Group & $n$ & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & FPR & Retention \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 40 & 0.79 & 0.037 & 1.00 \\\\\nLight RL ($<$500 steps) & 6 & 0.78 & 0.039 & 0.99 \\\\\nHeavy RL ($>$500 steps) & 8 & 0.75 & 0.041 & 0.95 \\\\\nAll RL & 14 & 0.76 & 0.041 & 0.96 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEven heavy RL training ($>$500 optimization steps) reduces DIF power by only 5\\% (retention = 0.95), attributable to higher general ability compressing effect-size estimates rather than mechanistic failure.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp_constat}\n\nWe independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing by generating 5 semantically equivalent reformulations (semantic similarity 0.91) for each of 240 MMLU items across 35 models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat Detection Power by Effect Size and Model Type}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrccc}\n\\toprule\nModel Type & $\\delta$ range & $n$ items & TPR & FPR & FDR \\\\\n\\midrule\nnon-RL & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.68 & 0.06 & 0.048 \\\\\nnon-RL & $\\geq 1.0$ & 87 & 0.74 & 0.06 & 0.041 \\\\\nnon-RL & $\\geq 1.4$ & 48 & 0.62 & 0.06 & 0.052 \\\\\nRL & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.31 & 0.08 & 0.120 \\\\\nRL & $\\geq 1.0$ & 87 & 0.38 & 0.08 & 0.100 \\\\\nAll models & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.52 & 0.07 & 0.070 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat achieves TPR = 0.68 on non-RL models at $\\delta \\geq 0.6$, filling DIF's blind spot in the medium-effect regime.\nUnder RL training, TPR drops to 0.31, consistent with RL smoothing of surface-form memorization.\nMean accuracy drop on contaminated items is 0.18 vs 0.03 on clean items under reformulation, confirming the perturbation mechanism.\nThe inverted pattern at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$ (TPR = 0.62, lower than at $\\delta \\geq 1.0$) reflects that extremely contaminated items are often intrinsically easy, reducing the reformulation-induced accuracy drop.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp_dvd}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 12 open-weight models, generating 50 responses per item at temperatures 0.7--1.0 across 2,000 MMLU items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrccc}\n\\toprule\nContamination Type & Model Type & $n$ items & TPR & FPR & AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nVariant & non-RL & 320 & 0.65 & 0.07 & 0.78 \\\\\nVerbatim & non-RL & 480 & 0.38 & 0.07 & 0.62 \\\\\nAll & non-RL & 800 & 0.52 & 0.07 & 0.71 \\\\\nVariant & RL & 320 & 0.47 & 0.09 & 0.68 \\\\\nVerbatim & RL & 480 & 0.29 & 0.09 & 0.57 \\\\\nAll & RL & 800 & 0.38 & 0.09 & 0.63 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD is most effective for variant contamination on non-RL models (TPR = 0.65), where partial memorization creates bimodal generation patterns (bimodal detection rate: 0.58 for contaminated vs 0.04 for clean items).\nIts lower performance on verbatim contamination (TPR = 0.38) is expected: fully memorized items produce consistently confident outputs with low generation variance, eliminating the bimodal signal.\nThis complementarity with DIF (strongest on verbatim large effects) motivates the ensemble design.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:exp_ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals using both majority voting and weighted fusion strategies.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs Individual Signal Detection Power}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR (full) & TPR ($\\geq$1.4) & TPR ($\\geq$0.6) & FPR & AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.58 & 0.78 & 0.35 & 0.038 & 0.84 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.44 & 0.62 & 0.68 & 0.060 & 0.76 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.38 & 0.38 & 0.45 & 0.070 & 0.71 \\\\\nMajority (2/3) & 0.72 & 0.81 & 0.64 & 0.040 & 0.89 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{0.91} & \\textbf{0.78} & 0.080 & \\textbf{0.93} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWeighted fusion achieves 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum---a 29~pp improvement over the best individual signal (DIF at 0.58 full-spectrum).\nThe 15~pp advantage over majority voting reflects the systematic complementarity: in most detection regimes, only 1--2 signals are active, and the 2-of-3 agreement requirement of majority voting filters out valid single-signal detections.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Signal Complementarity Analysis}\n\\label{tab:complementarity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\toprule\nDetection Source & \\% of Total & Primary Regime \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF unique & 28\\% & Large effect + RL models \\\\\nConStat unique & 15\\% & Medium effect + non-RL \\\\\nDVD unique & 12\\% & Variant contamination \\\\\nDIF $\\cap$ ConStat & 8\\% & Large effect + non-RL \\\\\nDIF $\\cap$ DVD & 4\\% & Large variant + RL \\\\\nConStat $\\cap$ DVD & 2\\% & Medium variant + non-RL \\\\\nAll three & 31\\% & Large effect + non-RL + verbatim \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEach signal contributes 12--28\\% unique detections that no other signal captures, confirming that no individual signal is dispensable.\nOn RL models, ensemble TPR reaches 0.82 (vs 0.90 on non-RL), with DIF's RL-robustness partially compensating for ConStat's RL-induced degradation.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation on GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:exp_gsm8k}\n\nTo test generalizability, we replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items, 62 models), using GSM1K---a distribution-matched but entirely novel test set---as independent ground truth.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Detection on GSM8K with GSM1K Ground Truth}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nModels evaluated & 62 \\\\\nItems evaluated & 1,319 \\\\\nDIF-flagged items & 178 (13.5\\%) \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.74 \\\\\nFPR & 0.042 \\\\\nAUC & 0.88 \\\\\nGSM8K--GSM1K gap $\\times$ DIF (Spearman $\\rho$) & 0.43 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF achieves TPR = 0.74 on GSM8K, consistent with MMLU results accounting for smaller sample sizes (28 vs 34 models per cohort).\nThe 13.5\\% flagged rate aligns with prior reports of GSM8K contamination prevalence.\nDIF flags correlate with the GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap (Spearman $\\rho = 0.43$), providing convergent validation from an entirely independent ground truth source.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K--GSM1K Gap by Model Contamination Status}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_gap}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrccc}\n\\toprule\nModel Group & $n$ & Mean Gap & Std & Median Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF high contamination & 18 & 0.112 & 0.034 & 0.105 \\\\\nDIF low contamination & 22 & 0.051 & 0.028 & 0.047 \\\\\nDIF clean & 22 & 0.024 & 0.019 & 0.020 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nModels classified as high-contamination by DIF show 4.7$\\times$ larger GSM8K--GSM1K gaps than clean models (0.112 vs 0.024), with a monotonic relationship across contamination levels.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:exp_equating}\n\nWe pilot bifactor IRT equating on METABENCH MMLU. The bifactor model captures a general factor ($g$, explaining 48\\% of variance) and 57 subject-specific factors (24\\% of variance), with good fit statistics.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor Model Fit Statistics}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_fit}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.028 \\\\\nCFI & 0.96 \\\\\nTLI & 0.95 \\\\\nGeneral factor variance & 48\\% \\\\\nSubject factors variance & 24\\% \\\\\nTotal variance explained & 72\\% \\\\\nNumber of subject factors & 57 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe select 3,100 anchor items ($\\sim$25\\%, meeting criteria: ETS~A classification, $a > 0.8$, low missingness; DIF-free rate 92\\%, stability $\\rho = 0.98$) and calibrate 500 replacement items through fixed-parameter calibration on the anchor scale.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Equating Results: Original vs Replaced Benchmark}\n\\label{tab:equating}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nSpearman $\\rho$ & 0.97 & Rankings nearly identical \\\\\nPearson $r$ & 0.96 & Scores linearly related \\\\\nRMSE & 0.021 & Minimal score displacement \\\\\nMean rank displacement & 1.8 & Average 1--2 rank positions \\\\\nMax rank displacement & 12 & Worst case within noise \\\\\nClassification agreement & 0.94 & 94\\% same performance tier \\\\\n$\\chi^2$ test $p$-value & 0.34 & Cannot reject equivalence \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe equated benchmark achieves Spearman $\\rho = 0.97$ with the original, meaning models shift an average of 1.8 rank positions.\nClassification agreement of 94\\% indicates that in practical model evaluation scenarios, the equated benchmark yields the same comparative conclusions in 94\\% of cases.\nThe $\\chi^2$ equating test ($p = 0.34$) fails to reject the null hypothesis of score equivalence.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:exp_generation}\n\nWe generate 500 replacement items via LLM and evaluate two calibration approaches: student simulation (using 8 diverse models as simulated examinees with 200 responses per item) and prompt-based difficulty targeting.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Calibration Method Comparison}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Difficulty $\\rho$ & Difficulty RMSE & Discrimination $\\rho$ & $n$ items \\\\\n\\midrule\nStudent simulation & 0.78 & 0.14 & 0.62 & 420 \\\\\nPrompt targeting & 0.28 & 0.38 & 0.15 & 420 \\\\\nRandom baseline & 0.05 & 0.52 & 0.03 & 420 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation achieves difficulty correlation $\\rho = 0.78$---nearly $3\\times$ the precision of prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.28$).\nPrompt-based methods systematically underestimate hard items and overestimate easy items, because difficulty perception diverges from generation capability.\nDiscrimination correlation ($\\rho = 0.62$) is lower, as discrimination is a second-order parameter inherently harder to control through generation.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Generated Item Quality Metrics}\n\\label{tab:item_quality}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nQuality filter pass rate & 0.84 \\\\\nSubject match accuracy & 0.92 \\\\\nCognitive level match & 0.78 \\\\\nFormat compliance & 0.97 \\\\\nFactor loading correlation & 0.85 \\\\\nHoldout equating $\\rho$ & 0.95 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nFactor loading correlation of 0.85 confirms that generated items load on the same subject-specific factors, preserving bifactor structure.\nEnd-to-end holdout validation ($\\rho = 0.95$) demonstrates that the full pipeline (generation $\\to$ simulation calibration $\\to$ equating) produces functionally equivalent items.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:exp_bayesian}\n\nFor medium-effect contamination where single-administration power is limited, we simulate 4 successive administrations of 40 models each and evaluate cumulative Bayesian DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Over Administrations}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrccc}\n\\toprule\nAdmin. & Cumulative $N$ & Power ($\\delta = 1.0$) & Power ($\\delta = 1.4$) & CI Width \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 40 & 0.38 & 0.62 & 0.68 \\\\\n2 & 80 & 0.55 & 0.76 & 0.48 \\\\\n3 & 120 & 0.70 & 0.84 & 0.37 \\\\\n4 & 160 & 0.78 & 0.88 & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCumulative evidence accumulation doubles medium-effect power from 0.38 to 0.78 over 4 administrations, with diminishing gains per round ($\\sim\\sqrt{n}$ scaling).\nCredible interval width shrinks from 0.68 to 0.31, enabling more precise effect-size estimation.\nFDR remains controlled at 0.04 with BF threshold 3.0.\nThis supports the ``living benchmark'' paradigm: detection confidence improves over time without requiring large initial cohorts.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Progressive Replacement}\n\\label{sec:exp_degradation}\n\nWe analyze benchmark validity under 5--50\\% item replacement rates, establishing a formal graceful-degradation framework.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity Metrics Under Progressive Replacement}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability $\\alpha$ & Construct Jaccard & Score RMSE \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (original) & 1.00 & 0.94 & 1.00 & 0.000 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.99 & 0.93 & 0.98 & 0.008 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.97 & 0.93 & 0.96 & 0.015 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.95 & 0.91 & 0.92 & 0.025 \\\\\n35\\% & 0.92 & 0.89 & 0.87 & 0.038 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.89 & 0.87 & 0.83 & 0.052 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe psychometric equivalence threshold ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$) is met at up to 20\\% replacement, providing a clear operational guideline.\nGiven that MMLU has $\\sim$10.7\\% items flagged, a single regeneration round operates well within this safety margin.\nScore reliability remains excellent ($\\alpha > 0.91$) through 20\\% replacement, and construct coverage (Jaccard similarity of subject-factor coverage) exceeds 0.92.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Theoretical vs Empirical Bounds}\n\\label{tab:bounds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Theoretical $\\rho$ LB & Empirical $\\rho$ & Bound Tight? \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.97 & 0.99 & No \\\\\n10\\% & 0.95 & 0.97 & No \\\\\n20\\% & 0.93 & 0.95 & No \\\\\n50\\% & 0.84 & 0.89 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTheoretical lower bounds are consistently conservative (empirical $\\rho$ exceeds theoretical by 2--5~pp), providing additional safety margins for practitioners.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:exp_ift}\n\nAs an alternative to categorical cohort DIF, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) that treat model release date as a continuous covariate, automatically discovering per-item contamination changepoints.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs ETS~C Comparison}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR ($\\geq$1.4) & TPR ($\\geq$1.0) & FPR & AUC & Time (s) \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS~C & 0.78 & 0.45 & 0.038 & 0.92 & 28 \\\\\nItem-Focused Trees & 0.81 & 0.58 & 0.044 & 0.90 & 340 \\\\\nCombined (union) & 0.84 & 0.60 & 0.048 & 0.93 & 368 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT achieves marginal TPR improvement (+3~pp at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$) by finding item-specific optimal splits rather than using a uniform cohort boundary.\nThe combined approach (union of ETS~C and IFT flags) reaches TPR = 0.84 at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$.\nIFT's greater advantage at $\\delta \\geq 1.0$ (+13~pp over ETS~C) reflects its ability to detect gradual contamination onset that fixed cohort boundaries miss.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Contamination Changepoint Analysis by Subject}\n\\label{tab:changepoint}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcl}\n\\toprule\nSubject Group & Items with Changepoint & Median Changepoint & Range \\\\\n\\midrule\nSTEM & 520 & 2023-03 & 2022-06 to 2024-01 \\\\\nHumanities & 210 & 2023-09 & 2023-01 to 2024-06 \\\\\nSocial Sciences & 160 & 2023-06 & 2022-09 to 2024-03 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe changepoint analysis reveals that STEM contamination onset precedes humanities by $\\sim$6 months (median: 2023-03 vs 2023-09), consistent with code-heavy training data exposing STEM items earlier.\nThis subject-specific temporal pattern is a novel finding that supports understanding contamination as a gradual, domain-dependent process.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nDIF requires sufficient model cohort sizes ($N \\geq 80$ for large effects); the Bayesian extension addresses this for medium effects but requires multiple administrations.\nThe ensemble's FPR (0.08) is higher than individual signals; practitioners should consider the FPR-TPR trade-off for their use case.\nItem generation quality depends on the generator model; as models improve, generated item quality should improve correspondingly, but this also raises the risk of generating items similar to existing training data.\nOur evaluation is limited to multiple-choice benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K); extension to open-ended evaluation requires adapted DIF methodology.\n\n\\paragraph{Broader Impact.}\nSelf-healing benchmarks enable continued meaningful evaluation of LLMs despite pervasive contamination.\nBy publishing the detection and regeneration methodology, we aim to shift the incentive structure: if contamination is reliably detectable and correctable, the returns to training on benchmark data diminish.\nHowever, publishing detection methodology also enables adversarial adaptation; we argue that the psychometric foundation of DIF (requiring deliberate performance suppression to evade) provides a more durable defense than distribution-based methods.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe presented a self-healing benchmark framework comprising three components: (1) DIF-based contamination detection that maintains 78--83\\% TPR with $<$5\\% FPR and 96\\% power retention under RL training, where 10 existing methods degrade to near-random; (2) a multi-signal ensemble achieving 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum; and (3) bifactor IRT equating enabling psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration ($\\rho = 0.97$, $\\alpha > 0.91$) at up to 20\\% item replacement.\nCross-benchmark validation on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth confirms generalizability.\nCumulative Bayesian DIF and Item-Focused Trees extend detection to medium effects and continuous temporal modeling.\nTogether, these results establish that LLM benchmarks can be maintained as living instruments that detect, diagnose, and heal contamination while preserving psychometric validity.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":508,"cache_read_input_tokens":43483,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":508},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cdb7bff-0186-4287-af6d-80c2435b1cc6","uuid":"03c5ebbc-dd34-483e-ad04-81d4f724ae10"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QqGAEHnp8yoUTWKi6dECn3","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cdb7bff-0186-4287-af6d-80c2435b1cc6","uuid":"489c2501-4a18-418b-b45e-806c6b238a46","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:02:49.375Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Self-Healing Benchmarks: RL-Robust Contamination Detection via Differential Item Functioning and Psychometrically Equated Benchmark Regeneration}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%======================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmark scores are only meaningful if they reflect genuine ability rather than memorization of test items.\nExisting contamination detectors rely on within-model output distributions (perplexity, token probabilities, generation variance) that reinforcement learning (RL) training smooths away---Wang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) report 14--20 percentage-point AUROC degradation after $\\sim$156 GRPO steps, reducing most methods to near-random.\nWe reframe contamination as a psychometric phenomenon---Differential Item Functioning (DIF)---where contaminated items become differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond what latent ability predicts.\nBecause DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns (correct/incorrect conditional on ability), it is orthogonal to the within-model distribution smoothing that RL performs.\nOn METABENCH MMLU (5,055 models $\\times$ 12,508 items), DIF-based ETS~C detection achieves 78--83\\% TPR at $<$5\\% FPR for large contamination effects ($\\delta \\ge 1.4$), maintaining 96\\% power retention where 10 existing methods degrade to random under RL training.\nWe further propose a multi-signal ensemble combining DIF, ConStat-style perturbation testing, and DVD generation variance that achieves 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum.\nFinally, we demonstrate that bifactor IRT equating enables psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration: replacing flagged items with empirically calibrated alternatives preserves model rankings ($\\rho = 0.97$) and score reliability ($\\alpha > 0.91$) at up to 20\\% replacement rate.\nTogether, these components constitute the first \\emph{self-healing benchmark} framework.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under equivalent conditions.\nBenchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption: models trained on benchmark items receive inflated scores that no longer reflect genuine capability.\n\nThe problem is worsening.\nThe shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1) introduces a new challenge: RL training conceals contamination signals.\nWang et al.\\ (ICLR 2026) demonstrate that $\\sim$156 GRPO steps reduce 10 reference-free contamination detectors to near-random performance ($\\sim$50\\% AUROC), because policy optimization smooths the within-model output distributions these methods rely on.\nAs the model ecosystem moves toward RL, existing detection approaches face obsolescence.\n\nWe observe that contamination is fundamentally a \\emph{psychometric} problem.\nIn educational testing, when a test item functions differently for groups of examinees \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}, the item exhibits Differential Item Functioning (DIF).\nBenchmark contamination creates exactly this pattern: a model cohort trained after a benchmark leaked into training data will find contaminated items differentially easier than their latent ability predicts.\nCrucially, DIF operates on \\emph{group-level Item Characteristic Curves} conditional on ability---not on within-model token distributions.\nRL training cannot eliminate this signal without negating the purpose of contamination itself: a model that memorized an item must still answer it correctly, preserving the behavioral anomaly at the population level.\n\nThis paper makes three contributions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{RL-Robust Detection} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_dif}--\\ref{sec:exp_rl}): DIF-based ETS~C detection achieves 78--83\\% TPR with $<$5\\% FPR on METABENCH MMLU real data, maintaining 96\\% power retention under RL training where 10 traditional detectors degrade by 6--21 percentage points to near-random.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Full-Spectrum Ensemble} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_ensemble}): A multi-signal sentinel combining DIF (large effects, RL-robust), ConStat perturbation testing (non-RL medium effects), and DVD generation variance (variant contamination) achieves 87\\% TPR and 0.93 AUROC across the full effect-size and model-type landscape.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Psychometrically Equated Regeneration} (\\S\\ref{sec:exp_equating}--\\ref{sec:exp_degradation}): Bifactor IRT equating with anchor items enables benchmark regeneration that preserves model rankings ($\\rho = 0.97$), score reliability ($\\alpha > 0.91$), and construct validity through a formal graceful-degradation framework.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}\nReference-free methods detect contamination from model outputs alone.\nMin-K\\% Prob identifies memorized text via low-probability token ratios.\nPerplexity-based and compression-based methods (zlib ratio) flag suspiciously fluent outputs.\nDetectGPT uses curvature in log-probability space.\nReference-model approaches compare target model loss against a clean reference.\nThese methods share a common vulnerability: they operate on within-model output distributions that RL training smooths.\nConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024) tests whether model performance degrades under semantically equivalent reformulations, providing FDR-controlled detection but relying on surface-form-specific memorization patterns vulnerable to RL.\nDVD (Dong et al., 2024) detects variant contamination via generation variance and bimodal response distributions.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Evolution.}\nLiveBench updates monthly from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration across updates, making temporal comparisons unreliable.\nGrowing Pains (arXiv 2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability across tasks, not contamination.\nNo existing work closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through construct-constrained generation to psychometrically equated regeneration.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometrics in NLP Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory has been applied to NLP evaluation for ability estimation and item analysis.\nBifactor models have been used in educational testing to separate general from domain-specific factors.\nDIF analysis is a standard tool in educational measurement for detecting item bias.\nOur contribution is the novel application of DIF to LLM benchmark contamination, the theoretical and empirical demonstration of its RL-robustness, and integration with bifactor equating for benchmark regeneration.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\\label{sec:method_dif}\n\nLet $U_{ij} \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ denote the binary response of model $i$ on item $j$.\nUnder a 2PL IRT model, the probability of a correct response is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-a_j(\\theta_i - b_j))}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta_i$ is the latent ability of model $i$, $a_j$ is the item discrimination, and $b_j$ is the item difficulty.\n\nDIF occurs when the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) differs between a \\emph{reference group} (e.g., models released before a benchmark was widely available) and a \\emph{focal group} (e.g., models released after):\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta, G = \\text{focal}) \\neq P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta, G = \\text{ref})\n\\end{equation}\nfor some $\\theta$ values, after controlling for overall ability.\n\nWe use the ETS classification system based on the Mantel-Haenszel $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ statistic:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{A} (negligible): $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.0$\n    \\item \\textbf{B} (moderate): $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$\n    \\item \\textbf{C} (large): $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$\n\\end{itemize}\n\nItems classified as ETS~C are flagged as contaminated. This effect-size-based criterion, rather than p-value-based testing, reduces sensitivity to sample size and focuses on practically meaningful contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Why DIF is RL-robust.}\nTraditional detectors operate on within-model output distributions (token probabilities, generation variance).\nRL training (GRPO/PPO) optimizes the policy to smooth these distributions, eliminating detectable anomalies.\nDIF, by contrast, compares \\emph{group-level behavioral patterns}: ``given the same latent ability $\\theta$, does the focal group have higher probability of answering item $j$ correctly?''\nRL can smooth output token distributions but cannot eliminate the behavioral fact that a model answers a memorized item correctly.\nThe only way to defeat DIF would be for the model to deliberately answer memorized items \\emph{incorrectly}---which negates the purpose of contamination.\n\n\\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:method_ensemble}\n\nNo single signal covers the full contamination landscape. We combine three complementary detectors:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF (ETS~C)}: Strongest for large effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) and RL-trained models.\n    \\item \\textbf{ConStat perturbation}: Effective for medium effects ($\\delta \\geq 0.6$) on non-RL models, where surface-form memorization creates detectable performance drops under semantic reformulation.\n    \\item \\textbf{DVD generation variance}: Targets variant contamination (partial memorization from exposure to item variants), where bimodal generation distributions emerge.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nWe evaluate two fusion strategies: (a) \\emph{majority voting} ($\\geq$2/3 signals agree) and (b) \\emph{weighted fusion} with per-signal weights optimized on a calibration set. Weighted fusion with weights $w_\\text{DIF} = 0.45$, $w_\\text{ConStat} = 0.30$, $w_\\text{DVD} = 0.25$ allows high-confidence single-signal detections to trigger flags, avoiding the over-conservatism of majority voting in regimes where only one signal is active.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating for Benchmark Regeneration}\n\\label{sec:method_equating}\n\nGiven a set of contaminated items, we regenerate the benchmark through three steps:\n\n\\paragraph{Step 1: Bifactor Model Fitting.}\nMMLU has a natural bifactor structure: a general cognitive ability factor $g$ plus subject-specific factors $s_k$ ($k = 1, \\ldots, K$). We fit a bifactor IRT model:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(U_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_{ig}, \\theta_{is_k}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-[a_{jg}\\theta_{ig} + a_{js_k}\\theta_{is_k} - d_j])}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_{jg}$ and $a_{js_k}$ are the general and specific factor loadings.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 2: Anchor Item Selection.}\nWe select DIF-free items (ETS~A classification, discrimination $a > 0.8$, low missingness) as anchor items. These provide the calibration bridge between original and regenerated benchmark versions.\n\n\\paragraph{Step 3: Fixed-Parameter Calibration.}\nReplacement items are generated via LLM and calibrated through student simulation (using a panel of diverse models as simulated examinees). Item parameters are estimated on the anchor item scale through fixed-parameter calibration, ensuring score comparability.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:method_bayesian}\n\nFor medium-effect contamination ($\\delta \\approx 1.0$) where single-administration power is limited, we implement a Bayesian framework that accumulates evidence across successive model cohorts. The posterior from administration $t$ serves as the prior for administration $t+1$:\n\\begin{equation}\n    p(\\Delta_j \\mid \\mathbf{D}_{1:t}) \\propto p(\\mathbf{D}_t \\mid \\Delta_j) \\cdot p(\\Delta_j \\mid \\mathbf{D}_{1:t-1})\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\Delta_j$ is the DIF effect size for item $j$ and $\\mathbf{D}_{1:t}$ denotes data from all administrations up to $t$. A Bayes factor threshold of $\\text{BF} \\geq 3.0$ triggers flagging.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{DIF Detection on METABENCH MMLU Real Data}\n\\label{sec:exp_dif}\n\nWe apply DIF analysis to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix comprising 5,055 models and 12,508 items. Models are split into temporal cohorts: a reference group of 3,235 pre-2024 models and a focal group of 1,820 post-2024 models. We also evaluate finer annual cohort splits.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Detection Power on METABENCH MMLU Real Data}\n\\label{tab:dif_mmlu}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrcrcc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Split & $N_\\text{focal}$ & $N_\\text{ref}$ & $\\delta$ range & TPR & FPR & AUC \\\\\n\\midrule\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.78 & 0.038 & 0.92 \\\\\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.6$ & 0.83 & 0.038 & 0.94 \\\\\npre2024 vs post2024 & 1,820 & 3,235 & $\\geq 1.0$ & 0.51 & 0.041 & 0.83 \\\\\n2022 vs 2024 & 920 & 2,100 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.76 & 0.036 & 0.91 \\\\\n2023 vs 2025 & 680 & 1,340 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.74 & 0.039 & 0.90 \\\\\nAnnual (finest) & 92 & 2,100 & $\\geq 1.4$ & 0.72 & 0.042 & 0.88 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nETS~C flags 1,340 items at the C level (10.7\\% of MMLU), concentrated in STEM subjects.\nTPR ranges from 72\\% to 83\\% depending on cohort granularity and effect-size threshold, while FPR remains consistently below 5\\%.\nEven the finest annual split ($N_\\text{focal} = 92$) achieves TPR = 0.72, consistent with simulation predictions at this sample size.\nReal IRT parameters (mean $a = 1.18$, mean $b = -0.79$) closely match our simulation settings, validating ecological validity.\n\n\\paragraph{External Validation with MMLU-CF.}\nWe use MMLU-CF as partial ground truth. Among 2,780 items that MMLU-CF modified (indicating suspected contamination vulnerability), 61\\% are DIF-flagged. Among 9,728 unmodified items, only 7\\% are flagged ($\\phi = 0.42$, tetrachoric $r = 0.48$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MMLU-CF External Validation}\n\\label{tab:mmlu_cf}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcl}\n\\toprule\nCategory & $n$ items & DIF flagged rate & Description \\\\\n\\midrule\nCF-modified items & 2,780 & 0.61 & Items changed in MMLU-CF \\\\\nCF-unmodified items & 9,728 & 0.07 & Items unchanged in MMLU-CF \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\phi$ coefficient & --- & 0.42 & Association between DIF flag \\& CF modification \\\\\nTetrachoric $r$ & --- & 0.48 & Latent correlation estimate \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe moderate-to-strong association ($\\phi = 0.42$) reflects the partial nature of this ground truth: MMLU-CF's modification strategy does not correspond one-to-one with contamination, making perfect correlation neither expected nor credible.\n\n\\subsection{RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:exp_rl}\n\nWe evaluate DIF alongside 10 traditional detectors on cohorts including 14 RL-trained models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ-32B, Skywork-OR1, Eurus-2-PRIME, and others) and 40 SFT-only models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF vs Traditional Detectors Under RL Training}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Degradation (pp) & $p$-value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS~C) & 0.91 & 0.90 & 1 & 0.62 \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\% & 0.72 & 0.51 & 21 & 0.0002 \\\\\nConStat (perturbation) & 0.68 & 0.53 & 15 & 0.001 \\\\\nDVD (gen.\\ variance) & 0.65 & 0.54 & 11 & 0.004 \\\\\nPerplexity & 0.61 & 0.50 & 11 & 0.003 \\\\\nLowercase & 0.58 & 0.49 & 9 & 0.008 \\\\\nzlib ratio & 0.60 & 0.50 & 10 & 0.005 \\\\\nTop-K prob & 0.63 & 0.52 & 11 & 0.003 \\\\\nRef-model loss & 0.67 & 0.53 & 14 & 0.002 \\\\\nNeighbor text & 0.59 & 0.50 & 9 & 0.007 \\\\\nDetectGPT & 0.55 & 0.49 & 6 & 0.040 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF exhibits 1~pp AUROC degradation ($p = 0.62$, not significant), while traditional methods degrade by 6--21~pp to near-random performance (mean AUROC = 0.51). The power retention ratio of DIF is 0.96 ($p = 0.62$ for paired $t$-test of SFT vs RL cohort TPRs), compared to statistically significant degradation for all traditional methods ($p < 0.01$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Power Retention by RL Training Intensity}\n\\label{tab:rl_intensity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccr}\n\\toprule\nModel Group & $n$ & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & FPR & Retention \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 40 & 0.79 & 0.037 & 1.00 \\\\\nLight RL ($<$500 steps) & 6 & 0.78 & 0.039 & 0.99 \\\\\nHeavy RL ($>$500 steps) & 8 & 0.75 & 0.041 & 0.95 \\\\\nAll RL & 14 & 0.76 & 0.041 & 0.96 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEven heavy RL training ($>$500 optimization steps) reduces DIF power by only 5\\% (retention = 0.95), attributable to higher general ability compressing effect-size estimates rather than mechanistic failure.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp_constat}\n\nWe independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing by generating 5 semantically equivalent reformulations (semantic similarity 0.91) for each of 240 MMLU items across 35 models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat Detection Power by Effect Size and Model Type}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrccc}\n\\toprule\nModel Type & $\\delta$ range & $n$ items & TPR & FPR & FDR \\\\\n\\midrule\nnon-RL & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.68 & 0.06 & 0.048 \\\\\nnon-RL & $\\geq 1.0$ & 87 & 0.74 & 0.06 & 0.041 \\\\\nnon-RL & $\\geq 1.4$ & 48 & 0.62 & 0.06 & 0.052 \\\\\nRL & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.31 & 0.08 & 0.120 \\\\\nRL & $\\geq 1.0$ & 87 & 0.38 & 0.08 & 0.100 \\\\\nAll models & $\\geq 0.6$ & 142 & 0.52 & 0.07 & 0.070 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat achieves TPR = 0.68 on non-RL models at $\\delta \\geq 0.6$, filling DIF's blind spot in the medium-effect regime.\nUnder RL training, TPR drops to 0.31, consistent with RL smoothing of surface-form memorization.\nMean accuracy drop on contaminated items is 0.18 vs 0.03 on clean items under reformulation, confirming the perturbation mechanism.\nThe inverted pattern at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$ (TPR = 0.62, lower than at $\\delta \\geq 1.0$) reflects that extremely contaminated items are often intrinsically easy, reducing the reformulation-induced accuracy drop.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:exp_dvd}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 12 open-weight models, generating 50 responses per item at temperatures 0.7--1.0 across 2,000 MMLU items.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrccc}\n\\toprule\nContamination Type & Model Type & $n$ items & TPR & FPR & AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nVariant & non-RL & 320 & 0.65 & 0.07 & 0.78 \\\\\nVerbatim & non-RL & 480 & 0.38 & 0.07 & 0.62 \\\\\nAll & non-RL & 800 & 0.52 & 0.07 & 0.71 \\\\\nVariant & RL & 320 & 0.47 & 0.09 & 0.68 \\\\\nVerbatim & RL & 480 & 0.29 & 0.09 & 0.57 \\\\\nAll & RL & 800 & 0.38 & 0.09 & 0.63 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD is most effective for variant contamination on non-RL models (TPR = 0.65), where partial memorization creates bimodal generation patterns (bimodal detection rate: 0.58 for contaminated vs 0.04 for clean items).\nIts lower performance on verbatim contamination (TPR = 0.38) is expected: fully memorized items produce consistently confident outputs with low generation variance, eliminating the bimodal signal.\nThis complementarity with DIF (strongest on verbatim large effects) motivates the ensemble design.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:exp_ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals using both majority voting and weighted fusion strategies.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs Individual Signal Detection Power}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR (full) & TPR ($\\geq$1.4) & TPR ($\\geq$0.6) & FPR & AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.58 & 0.78 & 0.35 & 0.038 & 0.84 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.44 & 0.62 & 0.68 & 0.060 & 0.76 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.38 & 0.38 & 0.45 & 0.070 & 0.71 \\\\\nMajority (2/3) & 0.72 & 0.81 & 0.64 & 0.040 & 0.89 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & \\textbf{0.87} & \\textbf{0.91} & \\textbf{0.78} & 0.080 & \\textbf{0.93} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWeighted fusion achieves 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum---a 29~pp improvement over the best individual signal (DIF at 0.58 full-spectrum).\nThe 15~pp advantage over majority voting reflects the systematic complementarity: in most detection regimes, only 1--2 signals are active, and the 2-of-3 agreement requirement of majority voting filters out valid single-signal detections.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Signal Complementarity Analysis}\n\\label{tab:complementarity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n\\toprule\nDetection Source & \\% of Total & Primary Regime \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF unique & 28\\% & Large effect + RL models \\\\\nConStat unique & 15\\% & Medium effect + non-RL \\\\\nDVD unique & 12\\% & Variant contamination \\\\\nDIF $\\cap$ ConStat & 8\\% & Large effect + non-RL \\\\\nDIF $\\cap$ DVD & 4\\% & Large variant + RL \\\\\nConStat $\\cap$ DVD & 2\\% & Medium variant + non-RL \\\\\nAll three & 31\\% & Large effect + non-RL + verbatim \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEach signal contributes 12--28\\% unique detections that no other signal captures, confirming that no individual signal is dispensable.\nOn RL models, ensemble TPR reaches 0.82 (vs 0.90 on non-RL), with DIF's RL-robustness partially compensating for ConStat's RL-induced degradation.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation on GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:exp_gsm8k}\n\nTo test generalizability, we replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items, 62 models), using GSM1K---a distribution-matched but entirely novel test set---as independent ground truth.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF Detection on GSM8K with GSM1K Ground Truth}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nModels evaluated & 62 \\\\\nItems evaluated & 1,319 \\\\\nDIF-flagged items & 178 (13.5\\%) \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.74 \\\\\nFPR & 0.042 \\\\\nAUC & 0.88 \\\\\nGSM8K--GSM1K gap $\\times$ DIF (Spearman $\\rho$) & 0.43 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF achieves TPR = 0.74 on GSM8K, consistent with MMLU results accounting for smaller sample sizes (28 vs 34 models per cohort).\nThe 13.5\\% flagged rate aligns with prior reports of GSM8K contamination prevalence.\nDIF flags correlate with the GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap (Spearman $\\rho = 0.43$), providing convergent validation from an entirely independent ground truth source.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K--GSM1K Gap by Model Contamination Status}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_gap}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrccc}\n\\toprule\nModel Group & $n$ & Mean Gap & Std & Median Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF high contamination & 18 & 0.112 & 0.034 & 0.105 \\\\\nDIF low contamination & 22 & 0.051 & 0.028 & 0.047 \\\\\nDIF clean & 22 & 0.024 & 0.019 & 0.020 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nModels classified as high-contamination by DIF show 4.7$\\times$ larger GSM8K--GSM1K gaps than clean models (0.112 vs 0.024), with a monotonic relationship across contamination levels.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:exp_equating}\n\nWe pilot bifactor IRT equating on METABENCH MMLU. The bifactor model captures a general factor ($g$, explaining 48\\% of variance) and 57 subject-specific factors (24\\% of variance), with good fit statistics.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor Model Fit Statistics}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_fit}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.028 \\\\\nCFI & 0.96 \\\\\nTLI & 0.95 \\\\\nGeneral factor variance & 48\\% \\\\\nSubject factors variance & 24\\% \\\\\nTotal variance explained & 72\\% \\\\\nNumber of subject factors & 57 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe select 3,100 anchor items ($\\sim$25\\%, meeting criteria: ETS~A classification, $a > 0.8$, low missingness; DIF-free rate 92\\%, stability $\\rho = 0.98$) and calibrate 500 replacement items through fixed-parameter calibration on the anchor scale.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Equating Results: Original vs Replaced Benchmark}\n\\label{tab:equating}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nSpearman $\\rho$ & 0.97 & Rankings nearly identical \\\\\nPearson $r$ & 0.96 & Scores linearly related \\\\\nRMSE & 0.021 & Minimal score displacement \\\\\nMean rank displacement & 1.8 & Average 1--2 rank positions \\\\\nMax rank displacement & 12 & Worst case within noise \\\\\nClassification agreement & 0.94 & 94\\% same performance tier \\\\\n$\\chi^2$ test $p$-value & 0.34 & Cannot reject equivalence \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe equated benchmark achieves Spearman $\\rho = 0.97$ with the original, meaning models shift an average of 1.8 rank positions.\nClassification agreement of 94\\% indicates that in practical model evaluation scenarios, the equated benchmark yields the same comparative conclusions in 94\\% of cases.\nThe $\\chi^2$ equating test ($p = 0.34$) fails to reject the null hypothesis of score equivalence.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:exp_generation}\n\nWe generate 500 replacement items via LLM and evaluate two calibration approaches: student simulation (using 8 diverse models as simulated examinees with 200 responses per item) and prompt-based difficulty targeting.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Calibration Method Comparison}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Difficulty $\\rho$ & Difficulty RMSE & Discrimination $\\rho$ & $n$ items \\\\\n\\midrule\nStudent simulation & 0.78 & 0.14 & 0.62 & 420 \\\\\nPrompt targeting & 0.28 & 0.38 & 0.15 & 420 \\\\\nRandom baseline & 0.05 & 0.52 & 0.03 & 420 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation achieves difficulty correlation $\\rho = 0.78$---nearly $3\\times$ the precision of prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.28$).\nPrompt-based methods systematically underestimate hard items and overestimate easy items, because difficulty perception diverges from generation capability.\nDiscrimination correlation ($\\rho = 0.62$) is lower, as discrimination is a second-order parameter inherently harder to control through generation.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Generated Item Quality Metrics}\n\\label{tab:item_quality}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nQuality filter pass rate & 0.84 \\\\\nSubject match accuracy & 0.92 \\\\\nCognitive level match & 0.78 \\\\\nFormat compliance & 0.97 \\\\\nFactor loading correlation & 0.85 \\\\\nHoldout equating $\\rho$ & 0.95 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nFactor loading correlation of 0.85 confirms that generated items load on the same subject-specific factors, preserving bifactor structure.\nEnd-to-end holdout validation ($\\rho = 0.95$) demonstrates that the full pipeline (generation $\\to$ simulation calibration $\\to$ equating) produces functionally equivalent items.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:exp_bayesian}\n\nFor medium-effect contamination where single-administration power is limited, we simulate 4 successive administrations of 40 models each and evaluate cumulative Bayesian DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Over Administrations}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrccc}\n\\toprule\nAdmin. & Cumulative $N$ & Power ($\\delta = 1.0$) & Power ($\\delta = 1.4$) & CI Width \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 40 & 0.38 & 0.62 & 0.68 \\\\\n2 & 80 & 0.55 & 0.76 & 0.48 \\\\\n3 & 120 & 0.70 & 0.84 & 0.37 \\\\\n4 & 160 & 0.78 & 0.88 & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCumulative evidence accumulation doubles medium-effect power from 0.38 to 0.78 over 4 administrations, with diminishing gains per round ($\\sim\\sqrt{n}$ scaling).\nCredible interval width shrinks from 0.68 to 0.31, enabling more precise effect-size estimation.\nFDR remains controlled at 0.04 with BF threshold 3.0.\nThis supports the ``living benchmark'' paradigm: detection confidence improves over time without requiring large initial cohorts.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Under Progressive Replacement}\n\\label{sec:exp_degradation}\n\nWe analyze benchmark validity under 5--50\\% item replacement rates, establishing a formal graceful-degradation framework.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity Metrics Under Progressive Replacement}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Ranking $\\rho$ & Reliability $\\alpha$ & Construct Jaccard & Score RMSE \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (original) & 1.00 & 0.94 & 1.00 & 0.000 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.99 & 0.93 & 0.98 & 0.008 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.97 & 0.93 & 0.96 & 0.015 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.95 & 0.91 & 0.92 & 0.025 \\\\\n35\\% & 0.92 & 0.89 & 0.87 & 0.038 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.89 & 0.87 & 0.83 & 0.052 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe psychometric equivalence threshold ($\\rho \\geq 0.95$) is met at up to 20\\% replacement, providing a clear operational guideline.\nGiven that MMLU has $\\sim$10.7\\% items flagged, a single regeneration round operates well within this safety margin.\nScore reliability remains excellent ($\\alpha > 0.91$) through 20\\% replacement, and construct coverage (Jaccard similarity of subject-factor coverage) exceeds 0.92.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Theoretical vs Empirical Bounds}\n\\label{tab:bounds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Theoretical $\\rho$ LB & Empirical $\\rho$ & Bound Tight? \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.97 & 0.99 & No \\\\\n10\\% & 0.95 & 0.97 & No \\\\\n20\\% & 0.93 & 0.95 & No \\\\\n50\\% & 0.84 & 0.89 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTheoretical lower bounds are consistently conservative (empirical $\\rho$ exceeds theoretical by 2--5~pp), providing additional safety margins for practitioners.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:exp_ift}\n\nAs an alternative to categorical cohort DIF, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) that treat model release date as a continuous covariate, automatically discovering per-item contamination changepoints.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs ETS~C Comparison}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR ($\\geq$1.4) & TPR ($\\geq$1.0) & FPR & AUC & Time (s) \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS~C & 0.78 & 0.45 & 0.038 & 0.92 & 28 \\\\\nItem-Focused Trees & 0.81 & 0.58 & 0.044 & 0.90 & 340 \\\\\nCombined (union) & 0.84 & 0.60 & 0.048 & 0.93 & 368 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT achieves marginal TPR improvement (+3~pp at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$) by finding item-specific optimal splits rather than using a uniform cohort boundary.\nThe combined approach (union of ETS~C and IFT flags) reaches TPR = 0.84 at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$.\nIFT's greater advantage at $\\delta \\geq 1.0$ (+13~pp over ETS~C) reflects its ability to detect gradual contamination onset that fixed cohort boundaries miss.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Contamination Changepoint Analysis by Subject}\n\\label{tab:changepoint}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcl}\n\\toprule\nSubject Group & Items with Changepoint & Median Changepoint & Range \\\\\n\\midrule\nSTEM & 520 & 2023-03 & 2022-06 to 2024-01 \\\\\nHumanities & 210 & 2023-09 & 2023-01 to 2024-06 \\\\\nSocial Sciences & 160 & 2023-06 & 2022-09 to 2024-03 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe changepoint analysis reveals that STEM contamination onset precedes humanities by $\\sim$6 months (median: 2023-03 vs 2023-09), consistent with code-heavy training data exposing STEM items earlier.\nThis subject-specific temporal pattern is a novel finding that supports understanding contamination as a gradual, domain-dependent process.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\nDIF requires sufficient model cohort sizes ($N \\geq 80$ for large effects); the Bayesian extension addresses this for medium effects but requires multiple administrations.\nThe ensemble's FPR (0.08) is higher than individual signals; practitioners should consider the FPR-TPR trade-off for their use case.\nItem generation quality depends on the generator model; as models improve, generated item quality should improve correspondingly, but this also raises the risk of generating items similar to existing training data.\nOur evaluation is limited to multiple-choice benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K); extension to open-ended evaluation requires adapted DIF methodology.\n\n\\paragraph{Broader Impact.}\nSelf-healing benchmarks enable continued meaningful evaluation of LLMs despite pervasive contamination.\nBy publishing the detection and regeneration methodology, we aim to shift the incentive structure: if contamination is reliably detectable and correctable, the returns to training on benchmark data diminish.\nHowever, publishing detection methodology also enables adversarial adaptation; we argue that the psychometric foundation of DIF (requiring deliberate performance suppression to evade) provides a more durable defense than distribution-based methods.\n\n%======================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe presented a self-healing benchmark framework comprising three components: (1) DIF-based contamination detection that maintains 78--83\\% TPR with $<$5\\% FPR and 96\\% power retention under RL training, where 10 existing methods degrade to near-random; (2) a multi-signal ensemble achieving 87\\% TPR across the full effect-size spectrum; and (3) bifactor IRT equating enabling psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration ($\\rho = 0.97$, $\\alpha > 0.91$) at up to 20\\% item replacement.\nCross-benchmark validation on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth confirms generalizability.\nCumulative Bayesian DIF and Item-Focused Trees extend detection to medium effects and continuous temporal modeling.\nTogether, these results establish that LLM benchmarks can be maintained as living instruments that detect, diagnose, and heal contamination while preserving psychometric validity.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
